Nethack purists will deride me for using this, but I don't think doing 
this is wrong.

How you do this will depend on what system you are using.  In my case, I 
am using Ubuntu 8.04.  If you are using a different system you will have 
to adjust the following for your system.

Basically, I wrote the following shell script

______________________
#! /bin/bash

while ([ "$contvar" != "no" ])
do
	cp -p /var/games/nethack/oldsave/*  /var/games/nethack/save/
	su bridean -c /usr/games/nethack-qt %1 %2 %3
	cp -p /var/games/nethack/save/* /var/games/nethack/oldsave
	echo "Continue? "
	read contvar
done
______________________

Basically what this does is take your save file and copy it to a 
different directory.  If you die, it will then take your old save file 
and put it back in your regular save directory.  Thus what you are 
effectively doing is restoring from a previous save.

I think doing this is moral for the following reasons:

1) Nethack already has a feature called "explore mode" where you can 
choose whether you want to die or not.

2) Many other great games, such as Starcraft, allow you to restore from a 
previous save.

3) If you are in a difficult situation, you can test various solutions to 
that situation without having to worry about dying and starting all over 
again.  Whereas in "explore mode" you can just simply hack your way out 
with your weapon and continuously saying 'n' to the question of whether 
you want to die or not.  Let me explain further:

Let's say you are a first level warrior going against a blue dragon.  
Each hit of your weapon causes 5 points of damage to the dragon whereas 
the dragon's hit causes 15 points of damage.  Your warrior has 20 hit 
points and the dragon has 120 hit points.

In explore mode you can hit the dragon two times, causing it to go down 
to 110 hit points and the dragon hits you two times and you die.  Die?  
N  Ok, so now you have 20 points and the dragon has 110 points.  You 
attack again and bring the dragon down to 100 points but the dragon kills 
you again.  Die?  N  So now you have 20 points again and the dragon has 
100 points, etc.  Eventually you will kill the dragon.

If you do the "restore save" method the above technique won't work 
because not only would you be restoring your warrior to 20 hit points but 
you would be restoring the dragon's hit points as well.  So, with 
"restore save" you are going to have to find a different solution besides 
just hacking your way through.  Maybe you could run away or teleport away?

I guess the one advantage over the "restore save" method as compared to 
the "explore mode" method is when you die, you can identify your items.  
So if you have a bunch of stuff you want to identify, go ahead and get 
killed on purpose.  Then identify the stuff and write it down on a piece 
of paper (or copy and paste it into a text file).  Then restore your last 
save.  Then you can go over your inventory and "name" all the stuff you 
just identified.

I would be interested in why you guys think that doing this is wrong.



